{
  "anomaly_detected": false,
  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [],
  "summary": "The netlist and SPICE deck are internally consistent and match every netlist-derived fact in the specification (power rails, buck/LDO feedback and timing values, isolated pH/EC I2C channels and their pull-ups, EZO_PDIS, TMC2209 coil/UART/STEP/EN nets, current-sense and VREF resistor values, float-switch pull resistors, and the ESP32 pin-to-net cross-check table in §4.4, which matches the build exactly). The few prose statements that appear to conflict (the component table calling T3 an N-MOSFET low-side ATO switch that 'drives ATO_VALVE', the power diagram showing +24V direct after the fuse, and the J1/J3 header confusion) are demonstrable authoring errors in the specification — AON6407 is a P-channel device and the spec itself mislabels its channel type, while the netlist's T3 is wired as a textbook high-side P-FET reverse-polarity switch (source=/+24V_F, drain=+24V, gate clamped by D3/R7) with the ATO solenoid actually switched low-side by T5 (AO3400A) via R10/T4, which is electrically sound and fulfils the README's reverse-polarity-protection claim. Remaining oddities (U1.EN left floating, U5's MS1/MS2/AD pins floating while U6/U7's are strapped, and the pump gate pull-up R5 to +3V3 giving a default-on circulation pump) are either consistent with the spec's silence, with the spec's own note that the firmware drives the pumps as plain GPIO and does not use the TMC2209 UART, or defensible fail-safe design choices, and do not by themselves indicate a deviation that makes the board behave incorrectly. No file-supported anomaly was found."
}

